# EVERY CLAIM FILED AS A POINT, BESIDE WHAT IT SAYS WOULD NARROW IT. One row per point claim
# anywhere in a document, `violates` where the answer is anything but `notApplicable`.
#
# ⭐ THE RULE: a point value files narrowsWhen as notApplicable, having no range. A figure with
#   no width has nothing to tighten, so any other answer is a sentence that cannot be acted on
#   -- a paste from a neighbouring claim, or a claim filed at the wrong width.
#
# ⛔ AND `unmeasured` IS A VIOLATION HERE TOO, WHICH A TEST FOR A STATED NARROWING CANNOT SAY.
#   Testing for a narrowing SENTENCE only, a point value answering "nobody has said what would
#   narrow this" passes. But that answer presupposes there is something to narrow: on a point
#   the question is not unanswered, it is inapplicable, and those are different facts.
#   ⚠️ THIS IS THE STRICTER OF THE TWO READINGS AND THE ROSTER SENTENCE IS WHY -- it says the
#     claim FILES notApplicable, not merely that it files no condition. All 94 point claims
#     in this corpus do. What would refute the strictness is a point value with an honest
#     reason to answer otherwise; `derived` would be the candidate, and `tests/state_coverage.rs`
#     has already ruled it incoherent for a narrowing, which leaves the strict form standing.
#
# ⛔⛔ RUN OVER `layer.demand_*` THIS RULE READS ⛔ VACUOUS. `narrowsWhen` is on `Claim` and
#    claims are everywhere, but reach a claim's width only from the table of the thing it
#    describes and the rule can only be written against demands, no one of which is a point
#    value in this corpus. It then examines nothing and says so, loudly, which is what the
#    coverage table is for. Against `pm.claim` it examines every point claim in every document.
-- pm:Claim/pm:narrowsWhen on any pm:Claim where low = high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.owns || ' claim ' || c.seq AS layer,
           c.narrows_absent IS DISTINCT FROM 'notApplicable' AS violates,
           format('%s exactly, and it still answers %s', c.mode,
                  coalesce(c.narrows_kind::text, 'absent: ' || c.narrows_absent)) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/point_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'narrows_a_point_value'
